            "content": "# Release Runbook\n\nThis monorepo publishes several independently-versioned packages to pub.dev:\n`core` (`flutter_widget_from_html_core`), the `fwfh_*` add-ons, and `enhanced`\n(`flutter_widget_from_html`, the \"everything bundle\" most users install).\n\n**Versioning of record is each package's own `packages/<pkg>/pubspec.yaml`.**\nThere is currently no repo-wide git tag kept up to date — tags stop at `v0.9.1`\nand are obsolete. Don't tag a release as part of this procedure; see\n\"Recommendation: reconsider tagging\" at the end if you want to change that.\n\nThis document was reconstructed empirically from real release history\n(`git log --oneline --all --grep=\"^Prepare\" -i`) — there was no earlier\ncorrect version of this file. A previous attempt at this file (commit\n`f73b7152`, \"chore: Release v0.17.2\") invented a different, incorrect\nprocess (see \"Mistakes found in the wild\" below); treat any doc other than\nthis one as unreliable and re-derive from `git log` if in doubt.\n\n## Packages, in publish/dependency order\n\n1. `packages/core` — `flutter_widget_from_html_core`\n2. `packages/fwfh_cached_network_image`\n3. `packages/fwfh_chewie`\n4. `packages/fwfh_just_audio`\n5. `packages/fwfh_svg`\n6. `packages/fwfh_url_launcher`\n7. `packages/fwfh_webview`\n8. `packages/enhanced` — `flutter_widget_from_html` (depends on all of the above)\n\nThis is also the order `tool/pub-publish.sh` publishes in, and the order\nrelease-prep commits are made in.\n\n## Step 1: Find every package with unreleased changes\n\nDo this for **every** package, not just the one you already know about — a\nrelease commonly bundles several unrelated packages.\n\nFor each package, find its last release-prep commit (the commit that last\ntouched its `CHANGELOG.md` — CHANGELOGs are only edited at release-prep time,\nnever during feature work, so this reliably marks \"last released state\"):\n\n```sh\ngit log -1 --format=%H -- packages/<pkg>/CHANGELOG.md\n```\n\nThen list what changed in that package's directory since:\n\n```sh\ngit log --oneline <that-sha>..origin/master -- packages/<pkg>/\n```\n\n**Not every commit that touches the directory warrants a release.** Skip (do\nnot bump, do not changelog):\n\n- Commits that only touch `packages/<pkg>/test/` — the `/test/` directory is\n  excluded from the published package (`pub-publish.sh` appends it to\n  `.pubignore`), so test-only or golden-image-only changes never affect what\n  ships.\n- Docs-only or formatting-only changes.\n- Anything under `demo_app/` or `.github/workflows/` — neither is published.\n\nA commit can touch multiple packages' `test/` dirs at once (e.g. a single\n\"fix flaky golden test\" PR) — check the actual file list per package, not\njust the commit's presence in the log.\n\n### The dependency-propagation trap\n\n`core` is a shared dependency of every `fwfh_*` add-on and of `enhanced`.\nEach `fwfh_*` package pins it with an **upper-bounded range**, not a caret:\n\n```yaml\nflutter_widget_from_html_core: \">=0.8.0 <0.18.0\"\n```\n\nThat upper bound is deliberately one past the *current* core minor version,\nso it excludes any hypothetical next-minor core release. This means:\n\n**Whenever `core` gets a new minor version, every `fwfh_*` package's upper\nbound is now too tight to admit it, and every one of them needs a release**\n— bump the upper bound to `<`(new core minor + 1)`.0` and cut at least a\npatch release for that add-on, **even if the add-on has no other changes of\nits own.** (Confirmed against `e54aa9d0`: core went 0.16.0→0.17.0 and every\n`fwfh_*` package got a same-cycle patch bump purely to widen this bound,\neach with a changelog entry like `Add support for\nflutter_widget_from_html_core@0.17.0 (#1475)` and no other change.)\n\nIf core only gets a patch bump, its existing minor stays the same, so no\ndownstream widening is needed.\n\n`enhanced` pins every dependency (including `core`) with an **exact caret**\n(`^0.17.2`, not a range) in `packages/enhanced/pubspec.yaml`. That means\n**`enhanced` needs a new version and a pubspec update whenever *any* one of\nits dependencies bumps** — there is no \"no other changes of its own\" escape\nhatch for `enhanced`; picking up a new dependency version is itself the\nchange.\n\n## Step 2: Decide each package's version bump (semver)\n\nApply standard semver per package, based on that package's *own* changes:\n\n- Only fixes (`fix:` / \"Fix ...\" commits) → **patch**.\n- Any new backwards-compatible capability (`Feature:` / \"Add ...\" commits)\n  → **minor** (this wins over any fixes bundled in the same release — take\n  the highest tier needed).\n- Breaking change → **major**. This should be rare and is worth flagging\n  explicitly when preparing the release, since none of the historical\n  \"Prepare\" commits examined contained one.\n- A bump that exists *only* to widen a dependency constraint (the\n  propagation case above, or an external dependency constraint widen like\n  `cached_network_image`'s) → **patch**. The package's own public API hasn't\n  changed; it's just now compatible with a wider range of a dependency.\n\n**`enhanced` is the one exception**: bump it to the *highest* tier among (a)\nits own direct changes and (b) every dependency it just picked up. A minor\nfeature added to `core` is a minor feature from an `enhanced` user's point of\nview too, even though `enhanced`'s own `lib/` didn't change. (Confirmed:\n`enhanced` has tracked `core`'s minor bumps 1:1 in every historical release\nwhere core bumped minor.)\n\n## Step 3: Update files, per bumped package\n\n| File | Change |\n| --- | --- |\n| `packages/<pkg>/pubspec.yaml` | Bump `version:`. If a dependency's constraint needs widening (Step 1), do that here too. |\n| `packages/<pkg>/CHANGELOG.md` | Add a new `## <version>` section at the top. |\n| `packages/<pkg>/README.md` | See the pin rule below. |\n| `packages/enhanced/pubspec.yaml` | For every *other* bumped package, update its dependency line to the exact new version (`^<new version>`). |\n\n### CHANGELOG conventions\n\n- New section header: `## <version>` (no `v` prefix, no date).\n- List **features/adds before fixes**.\n- Reference the driving PR number in parens: `(#1234)`. For a pure\n  dependency-propagation entry with no single driving change, cite the PR\n  that caused the *dependency* to need the bump (e.g. the feature PR that\n  bumped core to a new minor), not the release-prep PR itself.\n- Format: `Add support for <dep>@<version> (#1234)` for\n  compatibility/dependency-propagation entries — this exact phrasing is used\n  throughout history and should be kept for grep-ability.\n- Credit external contributors: `(#1234, authored by @username)`. Do **not**\n  add this for commits authored or co-authored by the maintainer\n  (`daohoangson`) even when an AI agent did the work — \"authored by\" is\n  reserved for genuinely external contributors, not the maintainer's own\n  tooling.\n- Don't include entries for the skipped commits from Step 1 (test-only,\n  docs-only, CI-only).\n\n### README version-pin rule (the most commonly-missed step)\n\nEvery package's own README shows an install snippet pinning itself, e.g.\n`packages/core/README.md`:\n\n```yaml\ndependencies:\n  flutter_widget_from_html_core: ^0.17.0\n```\n\n**This pin must always be the floor of the new version — `^<major>.<minor>.0`\n— never the exact new version.** Only touch it when the bump changes\n`major.minor`; a patch-only bump leaves the existing floor valid, so the\nREADME is untouched (no diff).\n\nThis is deliberate and is called out in `AGENTS.md`. It was also gotten\n**wrong** in the most recent real release: commit `f73b7152` (\"chore:\nRelease v0.17.2\") bumped `core` from `0.17.0` to `0.17.2` — a patch bump,\nsame minor — but changed both `packages/core/README.md` and\n`packages/enhanced/README.md` pins from `^0.17.0` all the way to `^0.17.2`.\nThat's a real bug in currently-committed history: those pins are needlessly\ntight and should have stayed `^0.17.0`. It doesn't need a standalone fix\n(the next minor bump supersedes it), but don't repeat it: **check whether\n`major.minor` actually changed before touching a README pin.**\n\nAdd-on packages that depend on `core` list it as `flutter_widget_from_html_core: any`\nin their own README (no pin at all) — there's nothing to update there.\n\n## Step 4: Commit shape\n\nOne commit per bumped package, in the dependency order from the top of this\ndocument, message format:\n\n```\n[<package_short_name>] v<version>\n```\n\nWhere `<package_short_name>` is the directory name under `packages/` (`core`,\n`fwfh_webview`, `enhanced`, etc). Examples: `[core] v0.18.0`,\n`[fwfh_webview] v0.15.8`, `[enhanced] v0.18.0`.\n\nOpen the PR with title:\n\n```\nPrepare v<version> releases\n```\n\nwhere `<version>` is `enhanced`'s new version (the \"main\" package version).\nLeave the PR body empty — GitHub's squash-merge will list every commit\nmessage as a bullet in the squashed commit body automatically, which is\nexactly the format seen in every historical \"Prepare vX releases\" commit\n(e.g. `078b98b8`, `fc6e7497`, `e54aa9d0`). Do not write your own PR body.\n\n## Step 5: Publishing\n\n**The person running this procedure prepares the commit(s)/PR only. Actually\npublishing to pub.dev is a separate, deliberate action the repo owner\n(\"the captain\") performs themselves — this runbook does not publish, and an\nagent following it should not run `flutter pub publish` or `tool/pub-publish.sh`\nto completion on the captain's behalf.**\n\nOnce the PR above is merged, `tool/pub-publish.sh` is the real mechanical\npublish step:\n\n- Runs in dependency order (same order as this document).\n- Per package: skips it if that pubspec version is already live on pub.dev\n  (safe to re-run / safe if only some packages actually needed a release).\n- Skips (with a warning) if the package directory has uncommitted git\n  changes.\n- Strips `dependency_overrides` from `pubspec.yaml` (pub.dev rejects them),\n  runs `flutter clean && flutter pub get`, runs `flutter test` if a `test/`\n  dir exists, generates a `.pubignore` from `.gitignore` (+`/test/`), removes\n  the `flutter:` key from `pubspec.yaml`, then `flutter pub publish`.\n- Reverts all of the above local mutations afterward (`git checkout .`) so\n  the working tree is left clean.\n- Requires `yq`.\n\n`tool/pub-get.sh` is unrelated to publishing — it's a convenience script for\na clean cross-package `flutter pub get` (deletes all `pubspec.lock`s and\nre-fetches, including `demo_app`). Useful after switching branches or before\nstarting a release, not a release step itself.\n\n## Recommendation: reconsider tagging\n\nThis is a **recommendation**, not existing practice — don't silently start\ntagging without deciding this deliberately. Currently nothing tags releases\nin git (tags stop at `v0.9.1`); the pubspec files are the sole source of\ntruth for \"what version is this\". A lightweight per-release tag (e.g.\n`v<enhanced-version>` pointing at the merged \"Prepare\" commit) would make\n`git log <tag>..HEAD` a reliable way to run Step 1 instead of the\nper-package \"last CHANGELOG-touching commit\" heuristic this document uses.\nThe heuristic works today and this doc doesn't assume tagging gets adopted.\n\n## Worked example\n\nThe `v0.18.0` release prepared alongside this revision of the runbook is a\nconcrete worked example of every rule above: a minor `core` feature forcing\na minor `core`+`enhanced` bump and a mechanical patch bump of every `fwfh_*`\nadd-on (dependency-upper-bound widening), plus one add-on\n(`fwfh_cached_network_image`) with its own independent compatibility fix\nlayered on top of that same mechanical patch bump. See the `[<pkg>] v...`\ncommits immediately following this one for the exact diffs.\n",
