            "new_string": "## Step 2: Decide each package's version bump (semver)\n\n**There is no clean, deterministic formula for minor-vs-patch in this repo's\nreal history.** It would be convenient if \"new capability → minor, fix only\n→ patch\" held, but it doesn't — checked against the actual releases:\n\n- `v0.17.2` (0.17.1→0.17.2) shipped two genuine new CSS features\n  (`text-emphasis`/`text-emphasis-style`, `text-decoration-style: wavy`) as\n  a **patch**.\n- `v0.14.12`-era releases shipped multiple new capabilities (roman-numeral\n  expansion, `text-shadow`, improved flex support) alongside fixes, also as\n  **patch**.\n- `v0.15.1` (0.15.0→0.15.1) raised the *minimum required Flutter version*\n  (3.7→3.10) — a real environment-constraint tightening — and still stayed\n  **patch**.\n- The two real minor bumps in recent history (`0.16.0`, `0.17.0`) each\n  coincided with a Flutter-floor raise, but that's not a clean rule either:\n  `v0.15.1` also raised the floor and stayed patch. So \"raises the Flutter\n  floor\" doesn't reliably predict minor vs. patch either.\n\nNet: minor-vs-patch has genuinely been maintainer discretion, not a\nmechanical semver rule, even for changes that read as \"features\" or\n\"floor-raises\" in the abstract. Don't assert a deterministic formula here —\napply this default instead:\n\n- **Default to patch**, even when a release includes new capabilities, fixes,\n  or a raised minimum Flutter/toolchain version. This matches the actual\n  majority pattern above.\n- **Escalate to minor only for a clear, deliberate reason** — e.g. the\n  maintainer explicitly wants to signal a notable capability, or there's a\n  real behavioral change users should notice before upgrading. If a release\n  spans several substantial features, that's a defensible case to escalate,\n  but it's a judgment call, not a rule — when unsure, ask the maintainer\n  rather than guessing.\n- **Major** is reserved for an actual breaking change (removed/incompatible\n  public API). None of the historical \"Prepare\" commits examined contained\n  one; treat it as rare and flag it explicitly for review rather than\n  deciding it alone.\n- A bump that exists *only* to widen a dependency constraint (the\n  propagation case above, or an external dependency constraint widen like\n  `cached_network_image`'s) → **patch**. The package's own public API hasn't\n  changed; it's just now compatible with a wider range of a dependency.\n\n**`enhanced` still needs a version whenever any dependency it exact-pins\nbumps** (see the propagation section above) — bump it to at least the same\ntier as the highest-tier dependency bump it's picking up this cycle. Since\nthe default posture above is patch, in practice this is usually also patch;\nescalate `enhanced` to minor only if you deliberately escalated one of its\ndependencies to minor for the reason above.",
            "old_string": "## Step 2: Decide each package's version bump (semver)\n\nApply standard semver per package, based on that package's *own* changes:\n\n- Only fixes (`fix:` / \"Fix ...\" commits) → **patch**.\n- Any new backwards-compatible capability (`Feature:` / \"Add ...\" commits)\n  → **minor** (this wins over any fixes bundled in the same release — take\n  the highest tier needed).\n- Breaking change → **major**. This should be rare and is worth flagging\n  explicitly when preparing the release, since none of the historical\n  \"Prepare\" commits examined contained one.\n- A bump that exists *only* to widen a dependency constraint (the\n  propagation case above, or an external dependency constraint widen like\n  `cached_network_image`'s) → **patch**. The package's own public API hasn't\n  changed; it's just now compatible with a wider range of a dependency.\n\n**`enhanced` is the one exception**: bump it to the *highest* tier among (a)\nits own direct changes and (b) every dependency it just picked up. A minor\nfeature added to `core` is a minor feature from an `enhanced` user's point of\nview too, even though `enhanced`'s own `lib/` didn't change. (Confirmed:\n`enhanced` has tracked `core`'s minor bumps 1:1 in every historical release\nwhere core bumped minor.)",
